WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.547
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.547 --> 00:00:06.385
The way memory works is
in terms of snapshots.

00:00:06.385 --> 00:00:09.379
I don't remember the entire experience.

00:00:09.379 --> 00:00:12.500
I remember moments in that experience.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.856
And why is that so critical because it
tells you that what you need to manage is

00:00:16.856 --> 00:00:19.730
not the film, but
it's specific kind of moment.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:23.226
What people most remembers
is that end overall,

00:00:23.226 --> 00:00:28.173
people rate colonoscopies that take
longer but end less painfully,

00:00:28.173 --> 00:00:33.135
better then colonoscopies that
are shorter but end more painfully.

00:00:33.135 --> 00:00:35.071
It doesn't matter how long something took.

00:00:35.071 --> 00:00:36.972
It matters how it ended.

00:00:36.972 --> 00:00:40.220
They remember the end, which we've talked
about, but they also remember the peak.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.735
It's something called the Peak &amp; End Rule.

00:00:41.735 --> 00:00:43.754
People take those two
pieces of information and

00:00:43.754 --> 00:00:45.579
that's how they remember an experience.

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:49.562
You can think of, you go to Disney
you go on the ride, that's an up and

00:00:49.562 --> 00:00:54.265
then you wait in line that's a down,
you go from sort of location, or location.

00:00:54.265 --> 00:00:58.387
It's not kind of a uniform experience,
it has all kinds of spikes, okay?

00:00:58.387 --> 00:01:03.591
And it has kind of moments in which
experiences naturally break up.

00:01:03.591 --> 00:01:05.856
So if you think in terms peak and end,

00:01:05.856 --> 00:01:10.841
you want to break up an experience when
you're near the top of something, right?

00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:14.961
So then when I think back as
a customer about my entire experience

00:01:14.961 --> 00:01:18.333
what I've coded in my head
is a series of peak modes.

00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:22.220
A lot of companies forget,
the importance of finishing strong.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.138
Hotels, they'll do a lot for
you when you come in, but

00:01:25.138 --> 00:01:28.698
then when you check out that
process isn't really managed right?

00:01:28.698 --> 00:01:32.881
It would actually be smart and appropriate
for them to give you a departure gift.

00:01:32.881 --> 00:01:36.685
So remember any kind of customer
experience that you have,

00:01:36.685 --> 00:01:41.493
go back to your desk after this webinar
and ask yourself, how do we finish?

00:01:41.493 --> 00:01:42.730
How does this thing end?

